    // ---- King of The Magnets VR ----
    "project.kotm.back": "Back to portfolio",
    "project.kotm.title": "King of The Magnets VR",
    "project.kotm.tagline": "Solo-developed VR game — use magnetic gloves to attract and throw weapons against hordes of enemies and solve puzzles.",
    "project.kotm.link.itch": "Play on itch.io",
    "project.kotm.link.steam": "View on Steam",
    "project.kotm.link.github": "Code on GitHub",
    "project.kotm.link.doc": "TFG Document",
    "project.kotm.about.heading": "About the project",
    "project.kotm.about.body": "This is the legend of the King of the Magnets. Whoever ventures into the King of the Magnets' dungeon, overcomes its challenges, and defeats him shall be known as the new KING OF THE MAGNETS! Use your magnetic gloves to solve puzzles, defeat enemies, and overcome challenges using your reflexes and your brain. Will you be worthy of becoming the next King of the Magnets?\n\nSolo personal VR project. The player uses magnetic gloves to attract and throw weapons, fight off hordes, and solve puzzles.",
    "project.kotm.role.heading": "My role",
    "project.kotm.role.body": "I was the sole developer on this project. I worked on everything from mechanics, gameplay, logic, and physics, to 3D modeling, texturing, and VFX. \n\nI implemented throwing and attraction mechanics in VR that, despite looking trivial, are internally quite complex, with plenty of challenges and math behind them, plus a couple of fun tricks. This mechanic handles curved throws, seamless aim assist, and the magnetic pull that brings weapons back to your hands like a boomerang. \n\nOther interesting challenges included: enemy AI and movement systems, combat and actor-interaction systems, and level scripting. Original gameplay and level design.\n\nThis project was my Multimedia Engineering degree's final thesis (TFG). It received an uncommonly awarded 'matrícula de honor' (highest distinction) for the research, development, and engineering behind it. You can read the document going into detail on the technical challenges and solutions behind this project through the links at the top of this page, or through the link below:",
    "project.kotm.role.document": "Download the TFG document: King of the Magnets VR",
    "project.kotm.challenges.heading": "Technical challenges",
    "project.kotm.challenges.item1": "A curved-throw system and aim assist that felt natural, easy, and fun to use.",
    "project.kotm.challenges.item2": "Coordinating movement, combat, and enemy AI around physics-based interactions in a VR experience.",
    "project.kotm.challenges.item3": "Designing levels and puzzles that made good use of the magnetic gloves.",
    "project.kotm.learned.heading": "What I learned",
    "project.kotm.learned.body": "I learned a huge amount about physics-based gameplay, since that was the main focus of this project. I learned to design fun experiences within the world of VR games, making something enjoyable, easy to understand, and satisfying to play. \n\nI also picked up a lot of things I don't usually get to work on in other projects, since I'm normally responsible for other areas — these new and interesting elements included: modeling and rigging, procedural animation, and art and texturing.",
    "project.kotm.facts.heading": "Fact sheet",
