    // ---- Get Out Of My Store! ----
    "project.goms.back": "Back to portfolio",
    "project.goms.title": "Get Out Of My Store!",
    "project.goms.award": "CPCRetroDev 2021-Best UA Game Winner",
    "project.goms.badge": "Assembly z80",
    "project.goms.tagline": "2D arcade top-down wave shooter. Developed in Assembly z80 for the Amstrad CPC 464.",
    "project.goms.link.itch": "Play on itch.io",
    "project.goms.link.steam": "View on Steam",
    "project.goms.link.github": "Code on GitHub",
    "project.goms.about.heading": "About the project",
    "project.goms.about.body": "Get Out Of My Store! is an arcade inspired top-down shooter where the objective is to survive enemy raids and get to the end. Every night is more difficult than the last, and new wacky monsters keep appearing until the very end. Can you survive all the nights? Refine your abilities, beat your hi-score, and triumphantly defend your store!\n\nThe game won 1st place in the UA category and 4th place in the overall category competing against industry professionals in the CPCRetroDev 2021.\n\nThis game was fully developed in Assembly z80 for the Amstrad CPC 464 with the use of the CPCtelera library.",
    "project.goms.role.heading": "My role",
    "project.goms.role.body": "We developed the full game and engine in Assembly z80 for the Amstrad CPC 464. Plain assembly with no use of AI, all hand written code: Gameplay mechanics, engine and physics, optimization and memory management, level systems, enemy AI, game design, art and music.\n\nFor the engine I built the entity system, game loop, and render system. The game engine supports 1 player entity, and 4-5 enemies with various behaviors on screen at the same time. (The game was initially supposed to be for 2 players, but due to time constraints, we had to limit it to 1. But the final game experience turned out really good.)\n\nI also built the physics engine that handled collisions, overlaps, bullets, enemy damage, and food system. I had to create a simplified version of a fixed point decimal system to handle the physics calculations so we could have a smooth movement at 50fps. This fixed point decimals also let us give the player and objects really specific movement speeds, which let us tweak the game feel to a very polished level, regardless of the machine limitations.",
    "project.goms.challenges.heading": "Technical challenges",
    "project.goms.challenges.item1": "Working within the Amstrad CPC 464's memory and performance limitations. Including handling memory, optimization, and performance profiling (with very archaic techniques unique to the Amstrad CPC 464).",
    "project.goms.challenges.item2": "Building the engine, physics, and level systems in Assembly z80. Fun fact: The Amstrad CPC 464 cannot perform multiplications natively.",
    "project.goms.challenges.item3": "Fitting gameplay, AI, art, and music into a three-month development cycle.",
    "project.goms.challenges.item4": "Another interesting challenge was to make the code fit within the 64KB memory limit of the Amstrad CPC 464. This meant we had to be extra careful with asset size, code size, and memory management. Every byte counts.",
    "project.goms.learned.heading": "What I learned",
    "project.goms.learned.body": "This is easily one of the most challenging projects I've worked on, but also the one that taught me the most. I learned to make a game engine from scratch with the most barebones tools possible.\n\nI learned how modern day game engines and systems work under the hood, to the byte level.\n\n I had to figure out and research how many data structures and algotorithms previously given for granted are actually implemented, and how to make the most of them.\n\nBut The most valuable experience I got was learning to design and implement a fun and award-winning experience within the strict constraints of a 1980s computer, working with very limited resources, both in time, tools, and technology.",
    "project.goms.facts.heading": "Fact sheet",
    "project.goms.facts.engine.label": "Engine",
    "project.goms.facts.engine.badge": "Custom engine",
    "project.goms.facts.engine.value": "Custom engine (Assembly z80)",
