        // ---- Samurai Squirrel ----
    "project.samuraisquirrel.back": "Back to portfolio",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.title": "Samurai Squirrel",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.tagline": "2D bullet hell roguelike dungeon crawler.",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.link.itch": "Play on itch.io",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.link.steam": "View on Steam",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.link.github": "Source code on GitHub",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.about.heading": "About the project",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.about.body": "A 2D bullet hell roguelike where you must make your way through a procedurally generated dungeon and reach the final battle. Fight with your sword, use smoke bombs, purchase upgrades, and defeat the Sniper Monkey.\n\nThis game was developed using a custom C++ engine built with the SFML library.",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.role.heading": "My role",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.role.body": "In this project, I worked on the development of the game engine. This included custom physics, a bullet system, map generation, entity management, memory management, optimizations, and enemy AI.",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.challenges.heading": "Technical challenges",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.challenges.item1": "Creating a 2D engine with its own game logic and physics systems.",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.challenges.item2": "Procedurally generating dungeons that meet specific design requirements and work correctly with the gameplay.",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.challenges.item3": "Keeping performance and memory usage under control with large numbers of enemies and projectiles. This included optimizations such as spatial partitioning to handle collisions with dozens of bullets on screen.",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.learned.heading": "What I learned",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.learned.body": "This was my first project in C++. Here, I learned a lot about engine architecture, physics, and memory optimization techniques. I also worked extensively on gameplay and game design, designing mechanics and enemies.\n\nI learned how to work as part of a relatively large team. This included version control and task delegation.",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.facts.heading": "Development details",
    "project.samuraisquirrel.facts.engine.label": "Engine",
