    // ---- Trashy Neighbours ----
    "project.trashyneighbours.back": "Back to portfolio",
    "project.trashyneighbours.title": "Trashy Neighbours",
    "project.trashyneighbours.tagline": "3D online party game. Fight as a raccoon using trash to defeat your neighbour.",
    "project.trashyneighbours.link.itch": "Play on itch.io",
    "project.trashyneighbours.link.steam": "View on Steam",
    "project.trashyneighbours.link.github": "Code on GitHub",
    "project.trashyneighbours.about.heading": "About the project",
    "project.trashyneighbours.about.body": "Fight your friends in a trash fight between two territorial raccoons. Make a gigantic ball of trash, throw it at your neighbour's house, and win the fight.\n\nThis is a 2-player online party-style PvP game. Both players roll balls of trash that get bigger and bigger, and the goal is to throw the trash at the opponent's house. The game was developed in less than 48 hours during a game jam and was built from scratch using Photon for the online system.",
    "project.trashyneighbours.role.heading": "My role",
    "project.trashyneighbours.role.body": "My role in this project was divided into 2 main areas: gameplay mechanics and the online system. I initially developed the movement mechanics, making the trash balls roll, grow in size, and creating the system for throwing them. After that, I focused on building the entire online system with Photon so that each player could play from different computers and from anywhere.",
    "project.trashyneighbours.challenges.heading": "Technical challenges",
    "project.trashyneighbours.challenges.item1": "Setting up a two-player online system with Photon from scratch in less than 48 hours.",
    "project.trashyneighbours.challenges.item2": "Synchronizing movement and gameplay actions during the match.",
    "project.trashyneighbours.challenges.item3": "Creating a movement system where the raccoons appear to drag the trash ball using physics to make it feel realistic.",
    "project.trashyneighbours.learned.heading": "What I learned",
    "project.trashyneighbours.learned.body": "In this project, the technical challenges of the online system speak for themselves. However, there was something more important that I learned from this project, which was a turning point for me.\n\nIn this project, I learned the importance of having a game loop as early as possible, and the priority that the core gameplay experience should have over all the additional polish. Let me explain. I spent most of my time on the online system while, in parallel, my teammates were working on the trash system that appears around the map, others were working on the art, and others were even making hazard systems for the map. In all this chaos, we made the mistake of not paying attention to the game loop. This meant that the game was not playable until the final hours of the jam. This was a real problem for balancing and testing. On top of that, this prioritization mistake made us spend a lot of time on aesthetic elements, hazards, and 'fun things' while the prototype still could not be played from beginning to end.\n\nThe most valuable lesson, which I have applied to all my projects ever since, has been to prioritize the game loop, even if it is not fun. Once you can complete a match, a level, whatever it may be, you can then focus on making the mechanics fun. Finally, once the game is playable and fun, that is when we add secondary systems to improve the experience, such as hazards, skins, extra abilities, etc.",
    "project.trashyneighbours.facts.heading": "Development details",
    "project.trashyneighbours.facts.engine.label": "Engine",
    "project.trashyneighbours.facts.engine.value": "Unity (C#)",
